      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 16,
        "titulo": "The imperfetto of description: age, time, weather and states",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: the imperfetto is the tense of the scene: it says what things were like, not what happened. STEP 2: age: «avevo dieci anni». Italian says «I had ten years», in the past as in the present, so never «ero dieci anni». STEP 3: time: «erano le otto di sera». With the hours, the verb is plural («erano») because the subject is «le otto». For one o'clock, the verb is singular: «era l'una». STEP 4: weather: «faceva freddo», «faceva caldo», «pioveva», «nevicava». Cold and hot weather use «fare»: never «era freddo». STEP 5: many physical states use «avere»: «avevo fame», «avevo sonno», «avevo freddo» (but «ero stanco» and «ero malato» use «essere»). STEP 6: description of people and places uses «essere»: «Era una casa piccola», «Ero molto contento». STEP 7: in a story, these descriptions set the scene before something happens: «Erano le otto di sera e faceva freddo». STEP 8: a state in the past with no clear end, such as «ero stanco» or «avevo paura», is also the imperfetto.",
        "comparacao": "English uses «was» and «were» for age, weather and time: «I was ten», «It was cold», «It was eight o'clock». Italian uses three different verbs for these three: «avevo», «faceva», «erano». Each English «was» needs a small decision. Time of day is the easiest to forget: «erano le otto» is plural, but «era l'una» is singular. If you remember only one thing, remember that hot and cold weather is «fare», not «essere».",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Faceva freddo e pioveva",
            "pt": "It was cold and it was raining"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Erano le otto di sera",
            "pt": "It was eight in the evening"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Avevo dieci anni",
            "pt": "I was ten years old"
          }
